  "answer": "Teriflunomide-Induced Differentiation in Pyrimidine-Salvage-Limited, Cytarabine-Persistent AML\n\nHypothesis: Within a subsequently verified human unbound-exposure window, teriflunomide will induce durable myeloid differentiation and reduce clonogenicity in salvage-limited, cytarabine-persistent AML through DHODH-dependent pyrimidine depletion, with less functional injury to normal hematopoietic progenitors. The drug-specific effect and salvage-based therapeutic selectivity are assumptions, not packet-established facts.\n\nSupported premises: DHODH inhibition promoted differentiation in experimental AML [S4]. Cytarabine-resistant model cells exhibited oxidative-metabolism-associated features without consistent enrichment for quiescent leukemia stem cells [S2]; they must not be equated with the ROS-low, BCL-2-associated population in S1 [S1]. Venetoclax–azacitidine is an established metabolic comparator, not a new repurposing discovery or evidence for teriflunomide [S3]. The speculative bridge is that insufficient salvage makes DHODH-dependent pyrimidine depletion release a differentiation blockade before generalized toxicity. Oxidative metabolism alone does not establish this dependency.\n\nFeasibility and novelty gaps: Unverified prior knowledge identifies teriflunomide as a DHODH inhibitor originally approved for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis. Approval, biochemical selectivity and human unbound exposure cannot be verified from this packet. Before advancement, require authoritative regulatory labeling, biochemical potency/counterscreen data, and human free-drug pharmacokinetic and tolerated-duration data. Check prior teriflunomide/leflunomide AML studies before claiming novelty; the candidate advance is biomarker/context selection, not the established DHODH–differentiation connection [S4]. Missing drug-specific sources remain unresolved gates rather than inferred support.\n\nPopulation definition: Study paired pretreatment and cytarabine-persistent primary AML specimens alongside normal donor hematopoietic stem/progenitor controls. Avoid assuming that low basal uridine incorporation proves limiting salvage: quantify maximal salvage flux across physiological uridine concentrations during a brief, nonlethal orthogonal DHODH challenge, relative to independently measured pyrimidine-replenishment demand. Normalize for cell number, proliferation, cell-cycle distribution and nucleotide-pool turnover; measure complementary de novo flux and respiration separately. Prespecify salvage-low as capacity covering <50% of demand and salvage-high as ≥80%; analyze intermediate specimens continuously. These are proposed experimental thresholds, not validated biomarkers. Compare all salvage/persistence strata and test whether persistence adds predictive value after adjustment for continuous salvage capacity, rather than assuming it does.\n\nExposure and response gates: Use physiological uridine/protein conditions and concentration–time profiles constrained by subsequently verified human unbound exposure and tolerated duration. Stop advancement if this envelope cannot produce ≥50% DHODH inhibition and ≥30% pyrimidine-pool depletion. Prespecify response versus vehicle as a ≥20-percentage-point increase in maturation, increased absolute counts of genotype-confirmed mature leukemic cells, and ≥50% loss of serial clonogenicity after washout, with ≥80% early viability. Require normal progenitors to retain ≥80% viability and colony formation at the same exposure. These exploratory decision rules do not establish a clinical therapeutic window.\n\nMechanistic discrimination: Track target engagement, pools, respiration, viability and maturation over time; require engagement/depletion to precede maturation. Require leukemic-genotype confirmation, morphology and single-cell lineage progression, not markers alone. Include vehicle, orthogonal DHODH inhibition, venetoclax–azacitidine and normal-cell comparators. Uridine rescue initiated before dosing must restore pools and prevent maturation; supplementation is a mechanistic control, not presumed clinically available protection. Genetically increase or suppress salvage capacity, verify altered tracer flux, and test reciprocal sensitivity changes without altered DHODH engagement. Develop and validate a catalytically competent, matched-expression, teriflunomide-resistant DHODH rescue rather than assuming one exists. Together these tests distinguish salvage causality and on-target differentiation from off-target killing, mitochondrial stress, selective survival of mature cells or normal-cell contamination.\n\nFalsification and translation: Across the admissible exposure envelope, confirmed target inhibition and depletion without durable differentiation or clonogenic loss in prospectively defined salvage-low residual AML would falsify the central prediction. Maturation persisting despite a pre-dose resistant-DHODH rescue that restores pools would reject the proposed mechanism; comparable normal-progenitor injury would reject selectivity. Absence of persistence-specific predictive value would preclude claiming a special residual-disease advantage. Advance only passing specimens to patient-derived models with measured unbound plasma/marrow exposure, differentiation, leukemia burden and marrow toxicity. Reduced leukemia-initiating activity requires transplantation evidence, not inference from differentiation. Marrow metabolite availability, heterogeneous salvage, culture-induced states and systemic toxicity remain limitations; even success would not establish clinical efficacy or universal stem-cell eradication.",
